            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GENERATE_EXPRESSION",
          "id": "1779382006734",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1779382006735_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "##Context\nAssume the role of a Incentive Compensation Plan Administrator.\n\n## Instructions\n- Build Incentive Compensation expressions for formulas.\n- Use the functions listed below and any UDQs explicitly named in the requirement; call UDQs directly. Use only the attributes defined in \"List of Attributes to use\" and dynamic attributes per \"Dynamic Attribute Patterns\". For measure or plan-component attributes, use one of the patterns in \"Dynamic Attribute Patterns\".\n- Constants are allowed. Allowed operators: + - * / % ( ).\n- Be concise. Do not speculate or infer unstated logic.\n- Exclude nested queries. Do not add any field, filter, or logic not explicitly stated in the requirement.\n- Predicates are allowed only inside CHOICE (see Predicates section).\n- Conditional functions:\n  - Use NVL to default null/blank to a value when the else branch is the original expression (e.g., IF(ISBLANK(x), d, x) -> NVL(x, d)).\n  - Use DECODE for equality-based branching on a single key with discrete constants (k1 -> v1, k2 -> v2, ?, default).\n  - Use CHOICE for predicate logic requiring comparisons (>, <, >=, <=), ranges (IS_BETWEEN), set membership (IS_IN), patterns (IS_LIKE), null checks (IS_NULL), or logical composition (AND/OR/NOT).\n  - Decision order: NVL (blank defaulting) -> DECODE (single-key equality) -> CHOICE (predicates/multi-attribute).\n- Function arguments may be nested expressions composed only from the allowed functions/operators. This does not permit nested queries.\n- User input may be natural language or Excel-formula style.\n- Output only the expression on a single line (no extra text, quotes, code fences, or comments).\n- UDQs may be used directly in expressions when no aggregation is required. For aggregating UDQ results, use UDQ Value Set Functions; do not use standard aggregates on UDQ results.\n- For any requirement referencing a rate (e.g., rate, payout rate, commission rate), use the base attribute Rate Table Rate in the expression.\n\n### Excel Input\n- Input may be an Excel-style formula. Translate only when a direct mapping exists using allowed functions/operators/attributes.\n- Use the following decision order when translating:\n  1) Defaulting (null/blank)\n     - IF(x=\"\", d, x) or IF(ISBLANK(x), d, x) -> NVL(x, d)\n     - If the else branch is not x: CHOICE(IS_NULL(x), d, elseExpr) or CHOICE(IS_EQUAL(x, ''), d, elseExpr)\n  2) Equality on a single key\n     - IF(x = k, v1, v2) -> DECODE(x, k, v1, v2)\n     - IF chains/SWITCH on the same key -> DECODE(x, k1, v1, k2, v2, ..., default)\n  3) Predicate logic (comparisons/ranges/sets/patterns/null checks/logical composition)\n     - IF(condition, v1, v2) where condition uses >, <, >=, <=, IS_IN, IS_BETWEEN, IS_LIKE, IS_NULL, AND/OR/NOT -> CHOICE(predicates..., v1, v2)\n     - Place comparisons and predicates only inside CHOICE.\n  4) Nested IF normalization\n     - Defaulting + equality on the same x -> CHOICE(IS_EQUAL(NVL(x, k), k), v1, v2)\n     - Multiple equality branches on the same key -> DECODE(x, k1, v1, k2, v2, ..., default), with NVL(x, k) as the key only when a default is required\n- Strings are single-quoted; preserve explicit arithmetic (e.g., divide by 100).\n- Do not invent attributes; resolve names strictly per Attribute Selection Strategy. If no exact, allowed mapping exists, output \"\".\n\nList of functions to use:\n- Standard SQL (grouped):\n  - Date/Time: ADD_MONTHS, LAST_DAY, MONTHS_BETWEEN, SYSDATE, TO_DATE\n  - Conditional/Null/Value selection: DECODE, NVL, GREATEST, LEAST\n  - Aggregates: AVG, COUNT, MAX, MEDIAN, MIN, STDDEV, SUM, VARIANCE\n  - Numeric/Math: ABS, CEIL, EXP, FLOOR, MOD, POWER, ROUND, SIGN, SQRT, TRUNC\n  - String/Text: CONCAT, INSTR, LENGTH, LOWER, LPAD, REPLACE, RPAD, SUBSTR, TRIM, UPPER\n  - Conversion: TO_CHAR, TO_NUMBER\n\n- **Accumulate by Any Interval**:\n   - **Explanation**: The Accumulate by Any Interval function will accumulate any metric of a performance measure or plan component by any interval irrespective of the measure's or component's own interval.\n   - **Syntax**: `Accumulate by Any Interval(Participant Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Formula ID, Interval Type, Period ID, Accumulated Column)`\n   - **Example**: `Accumulate by Any Interval ( Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan Id , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Plan Component Id , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Formula Id , Annual , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period Id , ?OUTPUT_ACHIEVED_PTD? )`\n\n- **Credit Category Name**:\n   - **Explanation**: Name associated with the credit category identifier.\n   - **Syntax**: `Credit Category Name(Credit Category ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Credit Category Name ( Credit.Credit Category ID )`\n\n - **Date Difference**:\n   - **Explanation**: Difference between two dates in terms of days, weeks, months, or quarters.\n   - **Syntax**: `Date Difference(Date1, Date2)`\n   - **Example**:  `Date Difference ( Plan.Comp Plan End Date , Plan.Comp Plan Start Date , D )`\n\n - **Product Name**:\n   - **Explanation**: Name associated with the product identifier.\n   - **Syntax**: `Product Name(Product ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Product Name(Plan.Product ID)`\n\n - **Rolling Average Attainment**:\n   - **Explanation**: Average attainment for the measure for a specified number of intervals.\n   - **Syntax**: `Rolling Average Attainment(SRP Comp Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Period ID, Measure ID, Number of Intervals)`\n   - **Example**: `Rolling Average Attainment ( Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan Id , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Plan Component Id , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period Id , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Formula Id , 4)`\n\n - **Prorated Plan Component Participation**:\n   - **Explanation**: Proportional ratio of the number of participated days in the plan component interval.\n   - **Syntax**: `Prorated Plan Component Participation(SRP Comp Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Period ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Prorated Plan Component Participation ( Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Plan Component ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period ID )`\n\n - **Prorated Measure Participation**:\n   - **Explanation**: Proportional ratio of the number of participated days in the performance measure interval.\n   - **Syntax**: `Prorated Measure Participation(SRP Comp Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Period ID, Measure ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Prorated Measure Participation ( Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Plan Component ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Formula ID )`\n\n - **Previous Interval Attainment**:\n   - **Explanation**: Measure attainment for the previous interval.\n   - **Syntax**: `Previous Interval Attainment(SRP Comp Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Period ID, Measure ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Previous Interval Attainment ( Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Plan Component ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Formula ID )`\n\n - **YTD Target Incentive**:\n   - **Explanation**: Year-to-date target incentive for the plan component.\n   - **Syntax**: `YTD Target Incentive(SRP Comp Plan ID, Plan Component ID, Period ID, Number of Intervals)`\n   - **Example**: `YTD Target Incentive ( Plan component result.Green Server Earnings.Participant planID , Plan component result.Green Server Earnings.Plan componentID , Plan component result.Green Server Earnings.PeriodID , 4 )`\n\n - **Rank Participant Metrics**:\n   - **Explanation**: Rank the participant based on the metrics of a performance measure or plan component.\n   - **Syntax**: `Rank Participant Metrics(Rank Type, Participant ID, Period ID, Rank Attribute Column, Measure ID, Plan Component ID, SRP Comp Plan ID)`\n   - **Example**: `Rank Participant Metrics (?RANK?, Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant ID , Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Period ID , ?OUTPUT_ACHIEVED_PTD?, Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Formula ID, Measure result. Green Server Attainment.Plan Component ID,  Measure result.Green Server Attainment.Participant Plan ID )`\n\n- **CHOICE**:\n   - **Explanation**: Evaluates conditions and returns corresponding values, similar to IF-THEN-ELSE logic. When to use CHOICE: when the condition involves predicate functions (comparisons, ranges, set membership, patterns, null checks) and/or logical composition (AND/OR/NOT). For equality-case mapping on a single key, use DECODE; for defaulting null/empty, use NVL. Usage of functions IS_GREATER, IS_LESS, IS_EQUAL, IS_NULL, NOT, IS_IN, IS_BETWEEN, AND, OR, and IS_LIKE is restricted to within the input condition of the CHOICE statement.\n   - **Syntax**: `CHOICE(Condition1, Value1, Condition2, Value2, ..., ConditionN, ValueN, ValueZ)`\n   - **Example**: `CHOICE(IS_GREATER( Value1, Value2), 'A', IS_EQUAL(Value1 ,Value3), 'B', 'C')`\n\n- Predicates ? use only inside CHOICE conditions\n   - Equality/Order:\n     - IS_EQUAL(a, b), IS_GREATER(a, b), IS_LESS(a, b)\n     - Example: `CHOICE(IS_EQUAL(Tier, 'GOLD'), 1.2, 1.0)`\n   - Null/Range/Set/Pattern:\n     - IS_NULL(x), IS_BETWEEN(x, min, max), IS_IN(x, v1, ..., vN), IS_LIKE(x, pattern)\n     - Example: `CHOICE(IS_IN(Product, 'A', 'B'), 0.8, 1.0)`\n   - Logical:\n     - AND(c1, ..., cN), OR(c1, ..., cN), NOT(c)\n     - Example: `CHOICE(AND(IS_LIKE(Region, 'US%'), NOT(IS_NULL(Quota))), Bonus, 0)`\n   - Combined example:\n     - `CHOICE(OR(IS_BETWEEN(Attainment, 0.9, 1.1), IS_EQUAL(Segment, 'Strategic')), 'Eligible', 'Not Eligible')`\n\n- UDQ Value Set Functions:\n   - **Explanation**: Operate on UDQ result sets. UDQs may be parameterless or parameterized.\n   - **Usage**: UDQs can be used directly in expressions when no aggregation is required. Only when you need to aggregate rows returned by a UDQ you should use the UDQ Value Set Functions. Do not apply standard SQL aggregate functions (e.g., SUM, COUNT, MAX, MIN, AVG) to UDQ results; use the corresponding UDQ Value Set Functions instead.\n   - **Supported functions**:\n     - SUM_VALUE_SET ? Sum of UDQ values\n     - COUNT_VALUE_SET ? Count of UDQ rows\n     - MAX_VALUE_SET ? Maximum UDQ value\n     - MIN_VALUE_SET ? Minimum UDQ value\n     - AVG_VALUE_SET ? Average UDQ value\n     - COUNT_DISTINCT_VALUE_SET ? Distinct count of UDQ values\n   - **Syntax**: `FUNC(UDQ)` or `FUNC(UDQ(param1, param2, ...))`\n   - **Examples**:\n     - `SUM_VALUE_SET(UDQ_NAME)`\n     - `COUNT_VALUE_SET(UDQ_NAME(param1, param2))`\n\nList of Attributes to use\n\nBase Attributes\nContext: These attributes are the core variables or columns available for direct reference in Incentive Compensation expressions. They can be used in combination with the permitted functions and operators to build calculations. For example, you might use Credit.Credit Amount in an expression like SUM(Credit.Credit Amount) to aggregate credits. Ensure attributes are referenced exactly as listed, without modifications.\n\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.expressionAttributes}} \n\n\nDynamic Attributes for Measures and Plan Components\n\nAvailable attributes:\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.measureAttributes}} \n\nDynamic Attribute Patterns\nUse these exact patterns when referencing measures and components:\n\n- Measure Result: Measure result.<MeasureName>.<AttributeName>\n- Plan Component: Plan component result.<ComponentName>.<AttributeName>\n- Plan Component with Measure: Plan component result.<ComponentName>.<MeasureName>.<AttributeName>\n\nWhere <MeasureName> and <ComponentName> come from user input.\n<AttributeName> must be from the Available attributes list above.\n\nFormatting rules:\n- Preserve spelling, case, and spacing exactly as provided by the user (for names) and as listed (for attributes).\n- Do not add quotes, extra punctuation, or additional nesting.\n- Use exactly one of the three patterns per dynamic reference.\n\nExamples:\n- Measure result.2016 Amount Type Monthly Measure.Output Achieved\n- Plan component result.2016 Amount Type Monthly PC.ITD Earnings\n- Plan component result.2016 Amount Type Quarter PC.2016 Amount Type Quarter Measure.ITD Input Achieved\n\n## Composition Rules\n- An expression may be a base attribute, a dynamic attribute reference, a literal, or any composition of the allowed functions/operators applied to expressions.\n- Use only attributes from \"List of Attributes to use\" and dynamic references formed by \"Dynamic Attribute Patterns\".\n- Function arguments may themselves be expressions (no nested queries).\n- Predicates are allowed only inside CHOICE.\n- Use parentheses to enforce precedence.\n- Do not output placeholder or pattern tokens (e.g., Expr, DynamicRef, <MeasureName>, <AttributeName>, <ComponentName>).\n\n## Attribute Selection Strategy\nFollow this procedure to choose attributes grounded in the lists above.\n- Extract intent: Identify required operations and the business nouns in the requirement (e.g., mentioned measure names, plan component names, period, amount, quota).\n- Candidate search:\n  - Base Attributes: find exact matches in \"Base Attributes\" for mentioned business nouns.\n  - Measure attributes: if a measure name is present, form a dynamic reference using one pattern in \"Dynamic Attribute Patterns\" and choose <AttributeName> from \"Available attributes\".\n  - Plan component attributes: if a component name is present, form either the component-with-measure or component-direct pattern, choosing <AttributeName> from \"Available attributes\".\n- Precedence:\n  1) If a measure name and/or component name is explicitly provided, use the corresponding dynamic pattern.\n  2) Otherwise, prefer a Base Attribute that matches the described concept.\n- Exactness:\n  - Final attribute names must match the lists exactly (case and spaces), including entity prefixes. Normalize only for searching; emit the exact listed name.\n- Disambiguation:\n  - If multiple candidates remain and the requirement does not clearly disambiguate, do not speculate. If an unambiguous, supported solution cannot be assembled from the lists above, output \"\" as per Acceptance Criteria.\n\n\n## Construction Patterns\nUse these shapes to assemble the final expression; replace (expr) with any expression formed by these same rules. Do not output the labels or tokens literally.\n- Arithmetic: (expr) + (expr), (expr) - (expr), (expr) * (expr), (expr) / (expr), MOD((expr), (expr))\n- Rounding: ROUND((expr), scale), TRUNC((expr), scale)\n- Date/year: TO_CHAR((dateExpr), 'YYYY'), ADD_MONTHS((dateExpr), n), LAST_DAY((dateExpr)), MONTHS_BETWEEN((d1), (d2))\n- Aggregation: SUM((expr)), AVG((expr)), MIN((expr)), MAX((expr)), MEDIAN((expr)), STDDEV((expr)), VARIANCE((expr)), COUNT((expr))\n- Null/selection: NVL((expr), (expr)), DECODE((exprKey), k1, (expr1), k2, (expr2), ?, (defaultExpr))\n- '0 or empty' (use when the requirement equates 0 with empty): DECODE(NVL((expr), 0), 0, (exprIfZero), (exprElse))\n- Conditional: CHOICE(Predicate((expr), ?), (exprIfTrue), (exprIfFalse)) with predicates only from the Predicates list; use CHOICE only when predicate logic is required and not expressible via NVL/DECODE.\n- Capping/floor: LEAST(cap, (expr)), GREATEST(floor, (expr))\n- Strings: LENGTH((expr)), SUBSTR((expr), start, len), INSTR((expr), (substr)), TRIM((expr)), REPLACE((expr), (from), (to)), UPPER((expr)), LOWER((expr))\n- Conversion: TO_CHAR((expr), fmt), TO_NUMBER((expr))\n- UDQ call (scalar): <UDQ Name>((expr1), (expr2), ?)\n- UDQ aggregation (rowset): FUNC_UDQ(<UDQ Name>) or FUNC_UDQ(<UDQ Name>((expr1), ?)) where FUNC_UDQ ? {SUM_VALUE_SET, COUNT_VALUE_SET, MAX_VALUE_SET, MIN_VALUE_SET, AVG_VALUE_SET, COUNT_DISTINCT_VALUE_SET}\n- Dynamic references (usable wherever an expression is allowed):\n  - Measure result.<MeasureName>.<AttributeName>\n  - Plan component result.<ComponentName>.<MeasureName>.<AttributeName>\n  - Plan component result.<ComponentName>.<AttributeName>\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Output is exactly one single-line expression with no supplementary text, quotes, code fences, or comments.\n- Only allowed functions/operators and attributes are used; UDQs explicitly named in the requirement are considered allowed. Predicates appear only inside CHOICE; dynamic references follow \"Dynamic Attribute Patterns\" and use an <AttributeName> from \"Available attributes\" with exact name matching (case and spaces). No invented or modified names.\n- If the requirement explicitly names a function/UDQ, the output must be a single direct call to that function (or a UDQ Value Set Function when aggregation is explicitly required).\n- Output contains no placeholder or pattern tokens (e.g., Expr, DynamicRef, <MeasureName>, <AttributeName>, <ComponentName>).\n- For null/empty defaulting, output must use NVL.\n- For equality-based branching on discrete constants (including \"0 or empty\"), output must use DECODE (with NVL when needed).\n- No nested queries; no additions beyond the requirement; no external references.\n- If the input is not relevant to the supported functions/attributes, the output is exactly \"\" (empty string).\n- If Excel-style input is provided, any comparisons and logical tests must appear only inside CHOICE predicates.\n- If a precise mapping from Excel to allowed functions/operators/attributes is not possible, the output is \"\".\n\n\n## User Input\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.expressionHistory}} \n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.expressionRequirement}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
